{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall present.</b> It may be explained along the lines of what they said at the beginning of tractate Keilim (chapter 1, mishnah 8) — that one who lacks atonement is forbidden to enter the Israelite courtyard, end of quote. Now here HaShem commanded that the one being purified should stand at the entrance of the courtyard, which is the Nicanor Gate, as they stated in the first chapter of Sotah (7a), for it is written ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״, and the verse states that it is incumbent upon the priest to guard him and stand him so that he does not break through, because he is at the entrance of the courtyard and inserts his hand inside to lean upon his offering, as they said in Torat Kohanim, and inserts his head inside to place [the blood] on the earlobe, etc. — there is reason to fear that perhaps he might be drawn in and enter inside. Therefore the verse placed the matter upon the priest to stand him." "<b>The priest who purifies.</b> It was necessary to say <b>the priest who purifies</b> and one of them alone would not have sufficed. If it had said ״the priest״ and not said ״who purifies,״ I would not know that the commandment falls upon the priest who purified him from the tsara'at — therefore it says <b>who purifies</b>, for his commandment falls upon the priest who purifies from tsara'at. And if it had said ״who purifies״ and not said ״the priest,״ since in the purification of the metzora there are things done by an Israelite, reason would lead one to say that even a non-priest would be called ״the one who purifies,״ since he too performs his purifications — Scripture comes to teach ״the priest.״" "<b>The man who is being purified, etc.</b> It was necessary to say <b>the man who is being purified</b> and it would not have sufficed to say ״the man״ alone, lest you err and say that it comes to exclude the minor who is not included in these laws, even though he is subject to the impurity of tsara'at. Therefore it says ״who is being purified.״ But it would not have sufficed to say ״who is being purified״ and not say ״the man,״ since minors too are included in this rite as mentioned; reason would lead one to say that HaShem did not command the priest to stand the metzora before HaShem — outside the Israelite courtyard — except one who is a minor, who presumably does not know that he may not enter the Israelite courtyard before bringing his purification offering, but an adult — one might say there is no obligation upon the priest to stand him, since he himself knows. Scripture comes to teach ״and he shall present the man״ — even though he is a man, it is incumbent upon the priest to stand him outside the courtyard." "And in Torat Kohanim they expounded the reason for His saying ״the man״ — a superfluous word — to exclude the sin-offering and the burnt-offering from waving, and afterward the verse stated another exclusion, for it is written ״אוֹתוֹ לָאָשָׁם״ — to exclude also the man, who does not require waving but only the guilt-offering and the log of oil alone. And if you will say: if the man is excluded from waving by the verse ״אוֹתוֹ לָאָשָׁם וְהֵנִיף״ and not the man, how can this come to exclude others regarding something in which [the man] is not included? This is no difficulty, for the exclusion falls upon them even if it is not the [expected] subject. And therefore you will find that the Tanna was precise in the baraita (Torat Kohanim here), and this is its language: Scripture comes to teach ״הָאִישׁ״ — not the sin-offering and not the burnt-offering — and he did not mention the remembrance of waving. His intention in this is to say that the word ״הָאִישׁ״ comes to exclude the sin-offering and the burnt-offering from one law, and even though the thing excluded from it is not [found] in the man, the matter is given over to the Sages to know what it is. And through this you will understand the meaning of this baraita in Torat Kohanim, which is difficult to understand. And the words of Ba'al Korban Aharon are far-fetched in my eyes."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:44.537374027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}